[339.251085, "o", "pdftotext DNA/1908.10275v1.pdf - | grep -i -C 2 -E \"Beethoven|Mozart|Bach|Jazz\"\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[339.281635, "o", "chants [7], that we classify half-way between world music\r\nand popular music. The first real popular music genres in\r\nthe 1900s were ragtime, pioneer of piano blues and \u001b[01;31m\u001b[Kjazz\u001b[m\u001b[K,\r\nand gospel, derived from religious chants of afro-american\r\ncommunities and pioneer of soul and RnB. The 1910s saw the\r\n"]
[339.281741, "o", "birth of tin pan alley (simple pop songs for piano composed\r\n\r\n\fby professionals) and dixieland \u001b[01;31m\u001b[Kjazz\u001b[m\u001b[K, a spontaneous melting\r\npot of ragtime, classical, afroamerican and haitian music [19].\r\nIn the 1920s, blues and hillbilly country became popular. The\r\n\u001b[36m\u001b[K--\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\ncommercialized tracks in the style of blues, generating boogiewoogie as a reaction, an underground and very aggressive\r\npiano blues played by black musicians. In Chicago and New\r\nYork \u001b[01;31m\u001b[Kjazz\u001b[m\u001b[K became more sophisticated and spread to Europe,\r\nwhere gipsy \u001b[01;31m\u001b[Kjazz\u001b[m\u001b[K became popular soon after. Both in US\r\nand Europe, the 1930s were dominated by swing, the most\r\npopular form of \u001b[01;31m\u001b[Kjazz\u001b[m\u001b[K, which was at the same time danceable,\r\nmelanchonic, catchy and intelligent. In the US the west swing,\r\na mellow and easy type of country music, became popular\r\nthanks to western movies. The 1940s in the US saw a revival\r\nof dixieland \u001b[01;31m\u001b[Kjazz\u001b[m\u001b[K, the rise of be-bop (one of the most mellow\r\nand intelligent forms of \u001b[01;31m\u001b[Kjazz\u001b[m\u001b[K), the advent of crooners (male\r\npop singers) and the establishment of back-to-the-roots types\r\nof country music such as bluegrass, a reaction against west\r\n\u001b[36m\u001b[K--\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\naudiences. Beside this, many things happened: urban blues\r\nforged its modern sound using electric guitars and harmonicas;\r\ncool \u001b[01;31m\u001b[Kjazz\u001b[m\u001b[K, played also by white people, launched a more\r\ncommercial and clean style; gospel influenced both doo-wop,\r\n(a-cappella music performed by groups of black singers imitating crooners) and RnB, where black female singers played\r\nwith a \u001b[01;31m\u001b[Kjazz\u001b[m\u001b[K or blues band. The 1960s saw an explosion of\r\ngenres: countrypolitan, an electric and easy form of country\r\nmusic, became the most commercialized genre in the US;\r\n"]
[339.282035, "o", "\u001b[36m\u001b[K--\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\naudiences; independent movements like beat generation and\r\nhippies helped the rise of folk rock and psychedelic rock\r\nrespectively [6]; funk emerged from soul and \u001b[01;31m\u001b[Kjazz\u001b[m\u001b[K (while \u001b[01;31m\u001b[Kjazz\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\nturned into the extremely complex free \u001b[01;31m\u001b[Kjazz\u001b[m\u001b[K as a reaction\r\nagainst the commercial cool \u001b[01;31m\u001b[Kjazz\u001b[m\u001b[K, but remained underground).\r\nIn the 1970s progressive rock turned psychedelia into a more\r\ncomplex form, independent radios contribute to its diffusion\r\n\u001b[36m\u001b[K--\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\nfor music marketing , exploited by glam rock, that emerged\r\nas a form of pop rock music with a fake trasgressive image\r\nand eclectic arrangements; fusion \u001b[01;31m\u001b[Kjazz\u001b[m\u001b[K begun to include funk\r\nand psychedelic elements; the disillusion due to the end of\r\nhippie movement left angry and frustrated masses listening to\r\n\u001b[36m\u001b[K--\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\nsynthetic sounds, that greatly improved the quality of records,\r\nyielding to one of the best selling genres in the whole popular\r\nmusic history. In a similar way smooth \u001b[01;31m\u001b[Kjazz\u001b[m\u001b[K (a mix of mellow\r\nand easy melodies with synthetic rhythmical bases) and soft\r\nadult (a mellow and easy form of pop) obtained a good\r\n"]
[339.289795, "o", "\u001b[36m\u001b[K--\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\ndedicated festivals and became mainstream with over-payed\r\nDJ-superstars [22]. In the underground remained various forms\r\nof nu \u001b[01;31m\u001b[Kjazz\u001b[m\u001b[K, hardcore techno, metal and house music. Then in\r\n2010s finally euro EDM house music (a form of samplebased and heavily danceable mix of house and electro) came\r\nout of underground communities and, borrowing the figure\r\n"]
[339.295616, "o", "\u001b[36m\u001b[K--\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\nconverts spontaneous novelties into commercial success, but\r\nwhen its products leave audiences frustrated (it happened with\r\nwest swing, glam metal, cool \u001b[01;31m\u001b[Kjazz\u001b[m\u001b[K, punk and many others),\r\nthey generate reactions in underground cultures, that trigger a\r\nchange into more aggressive versions of the genre. In general,\r\n"]
[339.295667, "o", "\u001b[36m\u001b[K--\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n0.15=garage/punk/hardcore, 0.2=rock, 0.25=pop\r\nrock, 0.3=blues, 0.4=country, 0.5=pop/traditional,\r\n0.55=gospel, 0.6=\u001b[01;31m\u001b[Kjazz\u001b[m\u001b[K, 0.65=latin, 0.7=RnB/soul/funk,\r\n0.75=reggae/jamaican,\r\n0.8=rap,\r\n"]
[339.302979, "o", "\u001b[36m\u001b[K--\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n\r\nWe computed the agreement between the two annotators using\r\nCron\u001b[01;31m\u001b[Kbach\u001b[m\u001b[K’s alpha statistics [23]. The average between all\r\nfeatures is α = 0.793, which is good. Among the most agreed\r\n\r\n"]
[339.304052, "o", "\u001b[36m\u001b[K--\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\nOriol Nieto and Juan Pablo Bello. Systematic exploration of computational music structure research. In ISMIR, pages 547–553, 2016.\r\nBruce Boyd Raeburn. New Orleans style and the writing of American\r\n"]
[339.30409, "o", "\u001b[01;31m\u001b[Kjazz\u001b[m\u001b[K history. University of Michigan Press Ann Arbor, 2009.\r\n"]
[339.3042, "o", "Peter J Rentfrow, Lewis R Goldberg, David J Stillwell, Michal Kosinski,\r\nSamuel D Gosling, and Daniel J Levitin. The song remains the same:\r\n\u001b[36m\u001b[K--\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\nTimothy D Taylor. Strange sounds: Music, technology and culture.\r\nRoutledge, 2014.\r\nP Wessa. Cron\u001b[01;31m\u001b[Kbach\u001b[m\u001b[K alpha (v1. 0.5) in free statistics software (v1. 2.1).\r\nOffice for Research Development and Education, 2017.\r\n\r\n"]
