[341.398281, "o", "\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[341.457534, "o", "\r\nFigure 1: Example of determining the “local key\" of each measure. The figure shows the first five measures from piano\r\n"]
[341.457769, "o", "sonata K. 545 in C by W. A. \u001b[01;31m\u001b[KMozart\u001b[m\u001b[K, along with the most likely key (or two most likely keys) determined by the CoE\r\nalgorithm..\r\nWe evaluated the accuracy our implementation of the CoE (Center of Effect), including the various weights we introduce\r\nin its implementation, by comparing its output to a gold standard of local keys annotated by hand, measure by measure,\r\nfor \u001b[01;31m\u001b[KBeethoven\u001b[m\u001b[K’s string quartets [33]. This annotated data is one of the most detailed set of such information in the\r\nliterature, it contains information about the key and chord in functional harmony for each measure in all \u001b[01;31m\u001b[KBeethoven\u001b[m\u001b[K’s\r\nstring quartets. Our CoE implementation matches the hand annotation in for more than 68.43% of the measures in the\r\ncorpus, and cases of mis-matches are often musically plausible alternatives (see SI).\r\n"]
[341.472046, "o", "\u001b[36m\u001b[K--\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n\r\nTo test the performance of our implementation of the center of effect model, we compared the results to hand-annotations\r\nof local key performed by musicologists, obtaining an accuracy of > 68.43% across all measures in \u001b[01;31m\u001b[KBeethoven\u001b[m\u001b[K’s\r\nstring quartets [33]. Some cases of mis-matches against this gold standard are necessarily incorrect example of calling\r\nthe local key (see supplementary S4). Overall, the ability to match hand annotations this often seems to validate\r\nour methodology, especially consider that professional musicians and musicologists may disagree about the key of a\r\nmeasure, even within the well structured \u001b[01;31m\u001b[KBeethoven\u001b[m\u001b[K quartets. It is somewhat useful, in addition, that the CoE algorithm\r\nprovides several alternatives, with associated likelihoods, for each measure – because the entropy of this distribution\r\nprovides a measure of key uncertainty, which is itself an interesting feature in music.\r\n"]
[341.475062, "o", "\u001b[36m\u001b[K--\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n3\r\n1720 Two part inventions - Invention #5\r\nJ.S. \u001b[01;31m\u001b[KBach\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n4\r\n1911\r\n"]
[341.485256, "o", "\u001b[36m\u001b[K--\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\nsame key – that is, focusing on harmonic transitions per se, after accounting for any possible changes in the tonic key of\r\nthe piece.\r\nSurprisingly, one of the least innovative composers according to this measure is W. A. \u001b[01;31m\u001b[KMozart\u001b[m\u001b[K. But it is well known\r\n"]
[341.485585, "o", "that \u001b[01;31m\u001b[KMozart\u001b[m\u001b[K’s style, like Haydn’s, is an archetype of the classical style, where clarity, balance and transparency are the\r\ncharacteristics of his work [53, 54]. It was until his late period of active years when he exploited chromatic harmony,\r\n7\r\n\u001b[36m\u001b[K--\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\none example is his popular String Quartet in C major also known as the “Dissonance\" quartet. Other observations\r\ncan be made from the results in figure 5, such as the change of novelty between the classical and romantic period,\r\nwhere \u001b[01;31m\u001b[KBeethoven\u001b[m\u001b[K (number 7, indicated in the figure) played an important role in the movement that consolidated with\r\ncomposers including Schubert and Liszt (numbers 8 and 9 respectively). The increasing trend in harmonic transition\r\ninnovation after the second half of the Classical period is a tipping point that led to subsequent continued increase in\r\n\u001b[36m\u001b[K--\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n1798\r\n7 Lander in D\r\n\u001b[01;31m\u001b[KBeethoven\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n0.1670\r\nI-I-I-V-I-I-I-V-I-I\r\n\u001b[36m\u001b[K--\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\nI-i-i-i-v-IV-I-i-I-IV\r\n1718 WTC I Prelude 14 in F# minor\r\nJ.S. \u001b[01;31m\u001b[KBach\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n2.1341\r\ni-i-#VI/bVII-i-#VI/bVII-#II/bIII-#II/bIII-v-v-v\r\n"]
[341.497637, "o", "\u001b[36m\u001b[K--\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\nnotably, sometimes all of the pieces by a given composer were assigned the same year, in [7]. Although ours results\r\nshare some similarities with [7], it also has its notable differences. For instance, Clementi is one of the least novel\r\ncomposers according to [7], while \u001b[01;31m\u001b[KMozart\u001b[m\u001b[K is the least novel in our study. B Both approaches show is a decline of novelty\r\nduring the Classical period, although is not clear if the explanation is the same, because the lack of transposition and\r\nreduction of octaves in [7] produce a fundamentally different novelty measure, that is influenced by variation in the\r\n"]
[341.500267, "o", "\u001b[36m\u001b[K--\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\ngeneration of appealing music. Physica A: Statistical Mechanics and its Applications, 389(1):126–132, 2010.\r\n[9] Leonardo Dagdug, Jose Alvarez-Ramirez, Carlos Lopez, Rodolfo Moreno, and Enrique Hernandez-Lemus.\r\nCorrelations in a \u001b[01;31m\u001b[KMozart\u001b[m\u001b[K’s music score (K-73x) with palindromic and upside-down structure. Physica A: Statistical\r\nMechanics and its Applications, 383(2):570–584, 2007.\r\n[10] Alfredo González-Espinoza, Gustavo Martínez-Mekler, and Lucas Lacasa. The arrow of time across five centuries\r\n\u001b[36m\u001b[K--\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n221(2):188–194, 2006.\r\n[12] Lu Liu, Jianrong Wei, Huishu Zhang, Jianhong Xin, and Jiping Huang. A Statistical Physics View of Pitch\r\nFluctuations in the Classical Music from \u001b[01;31m\u001b[KBach\u001b[m\u001b[K to Chopin: Evidence for Scaling. PLoS ONE, 8(3):1–6, 2013.\r\n[13] Jorge Useche and Rafael Hurtado. Melodies as maximally disordered systems under macroscopic constraints with\r\nmusical meaning. Entropy, 21(5), 2019.\r\n\u001b[36m\u001b[K--\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n[15] M. Beltrán del Río, G. Cocho, and G. G. Naumis. Universality in the tail of musical note rank distribution. Physica\r\nA: Statistical Mechanics and its Applications, 387(22):5552–5560, 2008.\r\n[16] Jack Murdoch Moore, Débora Cristina Corrêa, and Michael Small. Is \u001b[01;31m\u001b[KBach\u001b[m\u001b[K’s brain a Markov chain? Recurrence\r\nquantification to assess Markov order for short, symbolic, musical compositions. Chaos, 28(8), 2018.\r\n[17] Marco Buongiorno Nardelli. Tonal harmony, the topology of dynamical score networks and the Chinese postman\r\nproblem. arXiv, pages 1–21, 2020.\r\n[18] Luciano Telesca and Michele Lovallo. Analysis of temporal fluctuations in \u001b[01;31m\u001b[KBach\u001b[m\u001b[K’s sinfonias. Physica A: Statistical\r\nMechanics and its Applications, 391(11):3247–3256, 2012.\r\n[19] Alfredo González-Espinoza, Hernán Larralde, Gustavo Martínez-Mekler, and Markus Müller. Multiple scaling\r\n\u001b[36m\u001b[K--\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\nConference on Intelligent User Interfaces, Proceedings IUI, pages 227–236, 2009.\r\n[21] Fabian C. Moss, Markus Neuwirth, Daniel Harasim, and Martin Rohrmeier. Statistical characteristics of tonal\r\nharmony: A corpus study of \u001b[01;31m\u001b[KBeethoven\u001b[m\u001b[K’s string quartets. PLoS ONE, 14(6):1–16, 2019.\r\n[22] Tomas Scagliarini, Daniele Marinazzo, Yike Guo, Sebastiano Stramaglia, and Fernando E Rosas. Quantifying\r\nhigh-order interdependencies on individual patterns via the local o-information: theory and applications to music\r\n"]
[341.502582, "o", "\u001b[36m\u001b[K--\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n[29] D. Tymoczko. A Geometry of Music-Harmony and Counterpoint in the Extended Common Practice. Oxford\r\nUniversity Press, New York, 2011.\r\n"]
[341.502733, "o", "[30] Dan Wu, Keith M. Kendrick, Daniel J. Levitin, Chaoyi Li, and Dezhong Yao. \u001b[01;31m\u001b[KBach\u001b[m\u001b[K is the father of harmony:\r\nRevealed by a 1/f fluctuation analysis across musical genres. PLOS ONE, 10(11):1–17, 11 2015.\r\n[31] Jonathan Warrell, Leonidas Salichos, and Mark Gerstein. Latent evolutionary signatures: A general framework\r\n\u001b[36m\u001b[K--\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n[32] Marc Serra-Peralta, Joan Serrà, and Álvaro Corral. Heaps’ law and vocabulary richness in the history of classical\r\nmusic harmony. EPJ Data Science, 10(1):40, Aug 2021.\r\n[33] Markus Neuwirth, Daniel Harasim, Fabian C. Moss, and Martin Rohrmeier. The Annotated \u001b[01;31m\u001b[KBeethoven\u001b[m\u001b[K Corpus\r\n(ABC): A Dataset of Harmonic Analyses of All \u001b[01;31m\u001b[KBeethoven\u001b[m\u001b[K String Quartets. Frontiers in Digital Humanities,\r\n5(July):1–5, 2018.\r\n[34] David Temperley. What’s key for key? the krumhansl-schmuckler key-finding algorithm reconsidered. Music\r\n\u001b[36m\u001b[K--\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\nTENOR, Cambridge, (June), 2016.\r\n[52] R. Kamien. Music: An Appreciation. New York: McGraw Hill, 2008.\r\n[53] C. Eisen and Stanley S. The Cambridge \u001b[01;31m\u001b[KMozart\u001b[m\u001b[K Encyclopedia. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press., 2006.\r\n[54] D. Heartz. Music in European Capitals: The Galant Style, 1720–1780 (1st ed.). New York City: W. W. Norton &\r\nCompany., 2003.\r\n[55] Daniel J Levitin, Parag Chordia, and Vinod Menon. Musical rhythm spectra from \u001b[01;31m\u001b[KBach\u001b[m\u001b[K to Joplin obey a 1/f power\r\nlaw. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences, 109(10), 2012.\r\n[56] J. Wildridge.\r\n"]
[341.51739, "o", "\u001b[36m\u001b[K--\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\nof Chicago Press, 1967.\r\n[58] Brian Hyer. \"Tonality\". The Cambridge History of Western Music Theory. Cambridge University Press, 2002.\r\n[59] Philip G. Downs. Classical Music: The Era of Haydn, \u001b[01;31m\u001b[KMozart\u001b[m\u001b[K, and \u001b[01;31m\u001b[KBeethoven\u001b[m\u001b[K. W. W. Norton., 1992.\r\n[60] Charles Rosen. The Classical Style. W. W. Norton., 1972.\r\n[61] F. Tria, V. Loreto, V. D.P. Servedio, and S. H. Strogatz. The dynamics of correlated novelties. Scientific Reports,\r\n"]
[341.535786, "o", "\u001b[36m\u001b[K--\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n\r\nWe evaluate how accurate our implementation of the CoE (Center of Effect) algorithm performances by comparing the\r\nresults we get from \u001b[01;31m\u001b[KBeethoven\u001b[m\u001b[K’s string quartets with annotated ones from professional musicians [33]. This annotated\r\ndata is one of the most detailed set of musical analysis in the literature, it contains information about the key and chord\r\nin functional harmony terminology for each measure in all the \u001b[01;31m\u001b[KBeethoven\u001b[m\u001b[K’s string quartets. Our CoE implementation\r\nperforms accurately in more than 68.43% of the measures in the corpus provided by [33].\r\n\r\n\u001b[36m\u001b[K--\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n0.19\r\nTable S1: Results of the CoE algorithm for key-calling compared to a hand-annotated corpus [33]. We show this\r\n"]
[341.536181, "o", "comparison for the first ten measures of \u001b[01;31m\u001b[KBeethoven\u001b[m\u001b[K’s string quartet Op. 127. The hand-annotated key is denoted in\r\nthe column entitled “ Relative_Numeral\". Some measures have more than one posisble key in the annotated corpus,\r\nwhereas we show the most likely key per measure, according to the CoE algorithm.\r\n\u001b[36m\u001b[K--\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n\r\nI-I-I-v-I-I-I-v-I-I-I-v-I-II-v-I-ii-ii-ii-V-I-I-v-I-ii-ii-ii-V-I-I-vI-V-I-I-v-v-V-v-I-I-I-I-v-v-V-vI-I-ii-ii-ii-V-ii-ii-ii-V-I-I-v-v-Vv-I-I-I-I-I-I-I-I-I-I-I-I-I-I-I-I-I-IV-V-V-v-I-I-I-I-V-V-V-v-I-I-I-I-II-I-ii-I-i-I-v-I-I-I-ii-I-i-I-v-I-IVIV-V-I-IV-V-I-IV-IV-V-I-IVV-I-I-v-I-v-I-v-I-v-I-v-Iv-I-v-I-v-I-i-IV-i-IV-v-I-v-I7 Lander in D\r\n\u001b[01;31m\u001b[KBeethoven\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n0.1670\r\ni-IV-i-IV-v-I-v-I-I-I-V-I-II-V-I-I-I-V-I-I-I-V-I-IIV-I-v-I-IV-I-V-I-IV-Iv-I-IV-I-V-I-I-I-I-IV-V-V-V-I-I-I-I-V-V-VV-I-IV-IV-IV-V-I-I-V-IIV-IV-IV-V-I-I-V-II-I-i-I-I-I-I-I-I-I-II-I-I-I-I-I-I-I-i-I-i-II-I-I-I-I-I-I-I-I-I-i\r\n\u001b[36m\u001b[K--\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\nWTC I Prelude 14 in F# minor\r\n\r\nJ.S. \u001b[01;31m\u001b[KBach\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n\r\n2.1341\r\n"]
